Status: in review. Draft 3, circulated 2026-11-02 by Ines Kowalczyk, data platform lead. Comments close on 2026-11-20, and the plan goes to the engineering leadership review on 2026-11-25.

## Summary

This plan sets out what the data platform team will deliver in the first quarter of 2027 and what we are deliberately not doing. The short version is that Q1 is about three things. Workout events move from the nightly batch to a streaming pipeline so that the coaching features can use data from the same day. Warehouse spend comes down by at least 20% from its October 2026 level. Member deletion requests are handled end to end inside 14 days, including the copies in the lake and the warehouse, which today they are not.

Several things people asked for are not in this quarter. Real-time dashboards for the partnerships team, a self-serve feature store for the coaching models and the move of the marketing attribution jobs off the old scheduler all wait until Q2 at the earliest. The reasons are in the section on what we are not doing.

The team is seven engineers and one analytics engineer on loan from product analytics until the end of February. One engineer is on parental leave from mid-January to the end of March, so effective capacity for the quarter is about 6.3 people.

## Where we are after Q4 2026

Q4 went roughly to plan, with one big miss. The table lists what we committed to at the start of the quarter and where each item stands as of the end of October.

| Q4 commitment | Owner | Result | Notes |
|-|-|-|-|
| Move the sleep and recovery jobs to the new scheduler | Tomas Lindqvist | Done | 41 jobs moved, 3 retired instead of moved |
| Data contracts for the 10 most used event types | Aiyana Brooks | Partly done | 7 of 10 published, the other 3 wait on the mobile team |
| Cut warehouse spend by 10% | Ines Kowalczyk | Missed | Spend went up 6%, see below |
| Member deletion for the lake | Rahul Menon | Partly done | Deletes from the raw zone work, curated zone does not |
| Streaming pilot for workout events | Joon-ho Park | Done | Pilot ran on 5% of Android traffic for 4 weeks |
| On-call handbook for the platform | Femi Adeyemi | Done | Published in October |

Warehouse spend is the miss. We planned a 10% cut and spend went up 6% instead. Two things happened. The new year-in-review feature ran its aggregation queries against the full workout history for every member, every night, from late September until we caught it in mid-October, and a dashboard that refreshed every 5 minutes was left pointing at the unclustered events table. Both are fixed. The year-in-review queries alone cost about as much as the whole saving we had planned. The lesson we took from it is in the cost workstream below.

The streaming pilot went better than expected. End-to-end latency from the phone to the curated table was under 90 seconds at the 95th percentile, against a target of 5 minutes, and we lost no events that we could find in the reconciliation against the batch pipeline. The pilot also showed that about 1.2% of workout events arrive more than a day late, mostly from watches that sync only when the member opens the app.

### Incidents in Q4

There were three platform incidents in the quarter. None of them lost member data.

The longest was on 2026-10-03, when the nightly workout load failed after a schema change in the Android app added a field the loader did not expect. Coaching ran on two-day-old data for most of a Saturday, and about 9,000 members got a recovery suggestion that ignored their Friday workout. A data contract for that event would have rejected the change at the producer, which is part of why contracts are enforced in Q1.

On 2026-10-19 a watch bridge release sent each workout sample up to 40 times. The collector accepted all of them, and the raw zone grew by 3 TB in a night before the bridge team rolled back. The per-device rate limit described below came out of that incident.

The third was a permissions mistake on 2026-11-01, two days before this draft. A service account used by the BI tool was given read access to the restricted zone during a role cleanup. Nobody used it, and it was found and removed the same day by the weekly access review. It is the reason the access review moves from weekly to daily in January.

## Goals for Q1 2027

1. All workout events from iOS, Android and the three supported watch brands flow through the streaming pipeline, and the nightly batch for workouts is switched off by 2027-03-15.
2. Warehouse spend for March 2027 is at least 20% below October 2026, measured on the billing export, without removing any dashboard that has had a viewer in the last 60 days.
3. A member deletion request is complete in every store the platform owns within 14 days of the request, and we can prove it with a report for any single member.
4. Every event type with more than 1 million events a day has a published data contract.

Goals 1 and 3 are commitments. Goals 2 and 4 are targets we expect to reach, and we will say early if we cannot.

## Workstream 1: streaming workout events

### What changes

Today the phones and watch bridges send workout events to the collector, which writes them to object storage in hourly files, and a nightly batch job loads them into the curated workout tables at 03:00 UTC. Coaching features read from those tables, so a run done in the morning shows up in coaching only the next day. That is the single most common complaint in the app reviews that mention coaching.

In Q1 the collector writes every event to the event stream as well as to object storage. A stream processor validates events against their contract, removes duplicates and writes to the curated tables within minutes. The hourly files stay as the raw archive and the source for replays.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Workout event flow after the Q1 streaming change
  A[Phones and watch bridges] --> B[Collector]
  B --> C[Event stream]
  B --> D[Raw archive in object storage]
  C --> E[Stream processor]
  E --> F[Curated workout tables]
  E --> G[Dead letter topic]
  D -. replay .-> E
  F --> H[Coaching features]
  F --> I[Warehouse]
```

### Limits

The stream processor accepts events of up to 256 KB. Larger events go to the dead letter topic and are counted, not dropped. In the pilot, 0.03% of events were over the limit, all of them GPS-heavy ultramarathon workouts, and those are loaded from the raw archive by the batch job until we decide what to do with them.

Late events are accepted for 7 days after the workout ends. After that they are written to the raw archive only and do not reach the curated tables, except for events from members who have asked support to recover a missing workout, which support can replay by hand for up to 90 days. The collector rate limit is 50 events per second per device, which no real device comes near. It is there to stop a broken watch bridge from flooding the stream, as one did in August.

The stream is sized for 18,000 events per second at peak, which is about three times the busiest hour we have seen, the Sunday morning in January after the new-year campaign. Retention on the stream itself is 72 hours. Anything older has to be replayed from the raw archive.

### Cutover

Cutover runs one platform at a time, Android first, since the pilot was on Android. Each platform runs on both pipelines for at least 2 weeks, and the reconciliation job compares daily counts per member between them. A platform is switched to streaming only when the counts differ by less than 0.1% for 7 days in a row.

Double writing has been kept out of the cutover on purpose. We are not running the batch and the stream into the same tables at the same time, since the deduplication in the two pipelines works differently and members would see doubled workouts.

The nightly batch for workouts is switched off on 2027-03-15 at the earliest. It is not deleted. The job and its code stay in place, disabled, until the end of Q2, so that we can turn it back on if the stream processor has a bad week.

### Operating the pipeline

The stream processor becomes a tier 1 service on the day the first platform is cut over. Tier 1 means it is paged out of hours and has a 15 minute acknowledgement target, where the batch pipeline today is tier 2 and waits for the morning. The on-call rota stays at one person a week, and Joon-ho and Tomas will run two shadow weeks each before Android goes live so that everyone on the rota has seen the stream processor fail at least once in staging.

We will alert on three things. Consumer lag over 10 minutes for 5 minutes pages. The dead letter topic receiving more than 0.5% of events over 15 minutes pages. A difference of more than 1% in the daily reconciliation opens a ticket for the next working day but does not page, since a reconciliation gap does not hurt members on the day it is found.

Replays from the raw archive are run by the person on call and are limited to 7 days of data per replay, because a larger replay would compete with live traffic for the stream processor's capacity. A replay of more than 7 days is split into runs and done outside the Sunday morning peak. The stream processor writes replayed events with the same idempotency key as the original, so a replay that overlaps data already in the curated tables does not create duplicates.

The runbook for the stream processor does not exist yet. Writing it is part of the Android cutover, and the cutover does not happen until the runbook has been through one review by someone outside the team.

## Workstream 2: warehouse cost

:::decision{status=superseded date=2026-07-08}
Decision (Q3 2026 planning, 2026-07-08): the warehouse is retired by the end of Q1 2027. All curated tables move to the lakehouse on object storage using the open table format, the BI tool reads from the lakehouse query engine, and the warehouse contract is not renewed when it ends in April 2027. This is the main lever for cost and it is the cost workstream's only deliverable for Q1.
:::

The Q4 numbers changed that plan. A test of the BI tool against the lakehouse query engine in October found that 31 of the 120 most used dashboards were more than 5 times slower, and two of the finance dashboards did not finish at all. Fixing them is real work for the analytics engineers, who are not on this team.

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-10-21}
Decision (accepted 2026-10-21, engineering leadership): warehouse retirement is dropped from Q1 2027, and the warehouse contract is renewed for 12 months with the option to leave after 6. This replaces the decision from Q3 planning to retire the warehouse by the end of Q1. In Q1 the cost workstream cuts spend inside the warehouse instead, and the lakehouse move is planned again for the second half of 2027.
:::

The cuts we will make in Q1 are these.

- Cluster the four largest event tables by member and day. The October test showed this alone cuts scanned bytes for the top 50 queries by about 40%.
- Move tables that nobody has queried for 90 days to cold storage. There are 214 of them. Tables that a finance or compliance job reads are not moved, even if the job runs only once a year.
- Put a daily credit limit on each team's warehouse role. A query that would take a team over its limit is queued until the next day, except for the on-call role, which is never queued.
- Stop every dashboard refreshing more often than every 30 minutes, unless its owner asks for an exception and the platform lead agrees.

Any new scheduled query that scans more than 1 TB a run needs a review from the platform team before it goes live. This is the rule that would have caught the year-in-review queries.

### How the credit limits will work

Each team gets a daily credit allowance set at 110% of its average daily use in September and October, minus the year-in-review spike. Product analytics, finance and the coaching team have the three largest allowances, and together they account for about 70% of spend. The allowance resets at 00:00 UTC.

When a team reaches 80% of its allowance, the warehouse sends a message to the team's channel. At 100%, new queries from that team's role wait in a queue until the reset, and scheduled jobs that were already running finish. A team that hits its limit three days in a week can ask the platform lead for a higher allowance, and the request is decided within one working day. We would rather raise a limit than have a team copy data to a spreadsheet to get around it.

The finance month-end close is the one period where limits are lifted for the finance role, from the last working day of a month to the third working day of the next. Finance asked for this, and it costs less than arguing about it every month.

We expect complaints in the first two weeks. The limits start in report-only mode on 2027-01-11, where teams see the messages but nothing is queued, and they are enforced from 2027-01-25.

## Workstream 3: data contracts

A data contract is a short file, next to the producing code, that says what an event contains, who owns it and what consumers can rely on. The stream processor validates every event against its contract, so in Q1 contracts stop being documentation and start being enforced. Events that fail validation go to the dead letter topic.

Here is the contract for the workout summary event, the one most consumers read.

```yaml
event: workout_summary
version: 4
owner: team-tracking
pii: member_id
fields:
  member_id: {type: string, required: true}
  workout_id: {type: string, required: true}
  sport: {type: enum, values: [run, ride, swim, walk, strength, other]}
  started_at: {type: timestamp, required: true}
  duration_s: {type: int, min: 1, max: 172800}
  distance_m: {type: int, min: 0}
  avg_heart_rate: {type: int, min: 20, max: 250, sensitive: health}
compatibility: backward
```

A breaking change to a contract needs a new version, and the old version keeps being accepted for at least 30 days. Contracts can be published only by the owning team. The platform team reviews them but does not write them, except for the three event types that the platform itself produces.

The three event types still missing from Q4 are the watch sync event, the heart rate sample event and the app session event. All three belong to the mobile team, who have agreed to publish them in January.

### Turning on enforcement

Enforcement starts per event type, not all at once. For the first 2 weeks after a contract is published, the stream processor validates events against it and counts failures but still writes failing events to the curated tables. After that, failing events go to the dead letter topic. The owning team gets a weekly summary of failures during the 2 weeks and can ask for a short extension if the failures come from old app versions that members have not updated yet.

Old app versions are the hard part. About 4% of active members are on an app version more than six months old, and those versions send events that do not match the newest contracts. The processor accepts any contract version that is still inside its 30 day window, so most old versions are fine. App versions older than 12 months are not supported by the stream processor at all. Their events are written to the raw archive and loaded by a small batch job that stays in place after the main nightly batch is switched off.

## Workstream 4: member deletion and retention

### Deletion

When a member deletes their account or asks for their data to be erased, the account service publishes a deletion request. Today the platform deletes the member from the raw zone of the lake within 3 days, but the curated zone and the warehouse still hold the member's workouts until the tables are next rebuilt, which for some tables is never. That is the gap Q1 closes.

The deletion job will run every night, read the deletion requests of the last 24 hours, and delete the member's rows from every table that has a `member_id` column in the raw zone, the curated zone and the warehouse. Tables that hold only aggregates over 50 or more members are not touched, since a single member cannot be found in them. The job writes one line per member and table to the deletion log, which is the basis for the proof report in goal 3.

Deletions are never undone. Support cannot restore a deleted member's workouts, even if the member writes in the next day and says it was a mistake. The account service already warns the member about this twice before the request is sent.

:::danger
> **Important:** Raw health data, meaning heart rate samples, sleep stages and blood oxygen readings, must never be copied out of the restricted zone, not even for a one-off analysis or a deletion check. Members consented to that data being used to run their own coaching and nothing else, and a copy outside the zone puts us in breach of that consent and of the health data terms in two of our markets.
:::

### Access to the restricted zone

The restricted zone is the part of the lake that holds raw health data. Access is granted per person, for 30 days at a time, and renewing it needs a fresh approval from the coaching lead and the privacy officer. Service accounts get access only for named jobs, and the job's code has to be in a repository the platform team can review. At the start of November 11 people and 6 service accounts had access. The plan is to bring the people down to 6 by the end of Q1, mostly by giving the coaching analysts aggregate tables that answer the questions they ask most often.

From January the access review runs daily instead of weekly and posts any change to the restricted zone's access list in the security channel within an hour. Nothing about the review changes for the rest of the lake.

### Proving a deletion

Goal 3 asks for proof, not only deletion. Support and the privacy team get a report they can run for any member id, which lists every table the member appeared in on the day of the request, the time each was cleared and the run of the deletion job that cleared it. The report reads the deletion log, which is kept for 3 years and holds no member data beyond the id itself.

The report says "complete" only when every store has a log line. If any store is missing one after 14 days, the member appears on a daily list that goes to the person on call, who finds out why and fixes it. We expect a handful of these in the first month, mostly tables that nobody knew had a `member_id` column.

### Survey data and retention periods

Member research surveys run on Querent, a survey vendor. The research team exports responses into the lake every night so they can join them with anonymised usage data, and those copies follow the vendor's retention terms. This is the retention policy from the Querent agreement, and the lake applies the same periods to anything copied from it.

:::include{file=../_shared/data-retention.smd}
:::

Our research team has set survey responses to 60 days after each survey closes in their Querent workspace. The lake copy is deleted on the same day by a job the research team owns, and that job moves under the platform deletion job in Q1 so there is one place to check.

### Open question: model training snapshots

:::question
Undecided: whether a member deletion has to reach the training snapshots of the coaching models. The snapshots are frozen copies of curated tables taken when a model is trained, and they are kept for 18 months so that a model's results can be reproduced. Legal has been asked and expects to answer by the end of January. Until then, snapshots are kept as they are, no new snapshot may include a member whose deletion request is more than 14 days old, and the deletion log records which snapshots a deleted member appears in, so that we can go back and remove them if the answer is yes.
:::

## What we are not doing in Q1

Real-time dashboards for the partnerships team are not in Q1. They depend on the streaming pipeline, and we do not want a second set of consumers on it before the cutover is finished. The partnerships team keeps the hourly refresh they have now.

The self-serve feature store is not in Q1 either. The coaching team asked for it in September, and it is a good idea, but it is a quarter of work on its own and nobody on the team has built one. We will write a proposal in Q1 and ask for it in Q2 planning.

Health data access requests from outside the coaching team are not in Q1. Two product teams have asked for heart rate trends to use in their features, and the answer for now is the aggregate tables, which are outside the restricted zone and hold no row for any single member.

The marketing attribution jobs stay on the old scheduler. They run once a week, they work, and moving them gains nothing this quarter. The old scheduler is supported until the end of 2027, and only these jobs and the two finance exports still use it.

