## Dependencies on other teams

The plan needs work from four other teams. Each of them has seen the dates below and agreed to them in writing, except the account service team, whose Q1 planning finishes on 2026-11-27.

| Team | What we need | By | Status |
|-|-|-|-|
| Mobile | Contracts for the three missing event types | 2027-01-29 | Agreed |
| Mobile | Collector client update that sends to the stream | 2027-01-15 | Agreed |
| Account service | Deletion requests published with the request time and reason | 2027-01-08 | Waiting for their planning |
| Product analytics | Owners for the 31 slow dashboards named | 2026-12-15 | Agreed |
| Security | Review of the deletion job's access to the warehouse | 2027-01-12 | Agreed |

If the account service team cannot publish the request time by 2027-01-08, the deletion job uses the time it reads the request instead. That makes the 14 day clock up to one day shorter in practice, which is the safe direction.

## How we will report progress

Progress goes out every second Friday as a short note in the data platform channel, with four numbers in it. The first is the share of workout events going through the stream, by platform. The second is warehouse spend for the last 14 days against the same days in October. The third is the oldest deletion request not yet complete in every store. The fourth is the number of event types over 1 million a day without a published contract.

The first note goes out on 2027-01-15, and the last one for the quarter on 2027-03-26. A number that goes the wrong way two notes in a row gets a paragraph saying why and what we are doing about it. We will not change how a number is measured during the quarter, so that the notes stay comparable.

The engineering leadership review on 2027-02-17 is the mid-quarter checkpoint. If either commitment, streaming or deletion, looks likely to miss by then, we will say so there and propose what to drop.

## Risks

:::risk{impact=medium likelihood=high}
Risk: the watch bridges for two of the three watch brands batch their uploads and can send a whole week of workouts at once after a long gap. Impact medium, likelihood high. A burst like that from many members at the same time, for example after a bridge update, could push the stream processor past its peak sizing. The mitigation is the per-device rate limit and an autoscaling rule that we will load test in January.
:::

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=medium}
Risk: the cost cuts depend on Maren, who leaves at the end of February. Impact high, likelihood medium. If the clustering and cold storage work is not done by then, the 20% target is unlikely. We have front-loaded her work and asked product analytics whether the loan can run to mid-March.
:::

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=low}
Risk: the deletion job deletes rows that a legal hold should have kept. Impact high, likelihood low. The job will read the legal hold list before every run and skip any member on it, and the first two weeks of runs will be in report-only mode, where the job logs what it would delete and deletes nothing.
:::

