## Background: how the platform got here {agent=skip}

This section is for people new to the team or reading the plan from outside it. Nothing in it changes what we will do in Q1.

The data platform started in 2021 as one engineer and a set of scripts that copied the production database into a warehouse every night. Stridewell then had about 200,000 members and one app. The warehouse was chosen because the analytics team already knew it, and for two years it was cheap enough that nobody looked at the bill. By 2023 there were 3 million members, two watch integrations and a coaching product, and the nightly copy had become 400 jobs on a scheduler nobody wanted to own. The platform team was formed in early 2024 to own it.

Most of what the team has done since then is untangling. The scheduler migration, which finished in Q4, removed the last of the jobs that read from the production database directly. The lakehouse idea came from a cost review in mid-2025, when warehouse spend passed the cost of the whole team, and it is still the long-term plan. It was dropped from Q1 because of the dashboards, not because anyone changed their mind about it.

Member deletion has a longer history than most people expect. The first version, in 2022, deleted the member from the production database and nowhere else, on the assumption that the warehouse would catch up on the next full copy. Full copies stopped in 2023 when the tables became too large, and the gap went unnoticed until a privacy audit in early 2026 found workouts from members who had left two years before. The raw zone fix in Q4 was the first part of the answer, and the Q1 work is the rest of it.

### Notes from the planning session on 2026-10-29

Present: Ines, Joon-ho, Tomas, Aiyana, Rahul, Femi, Grace and Maren, with Priyanka from coaching for the first hour.

Priyanka made the case for the feature store again and offered one of her engineers for it. We said no for Q1, mostly because the streaming cutover is already the riskiest thing we have done, and we did not want a second new system in the same quarter. She was disappointed but agreed to write the requirements with us in Q1.

Femi asked whether the 20% target is realistic without the lakehouse. Maren's estimate is 22 to 28% from clustering, cold storage and the refresh limits together, which leaves little room if the credit limits annoy people and we have to relax them. We kept 20% as the target and agreed to report the number every two weeks.

Rahul raised the training snapshots question, which is now the open question above. Grace pointed out that the coaching team keeps some snapshots on their own laptops for debugging. That is being handled separately with the security team and is not part of this plan.
